/-
# Exact mean and variance of overlapping pattern counts

Régnier and Szpankowski (1998): let `H = h_1 … h_m` be a pattern of length `m ≥ 1` over a finite
alphabet and `O_n(H)` the number of (possibly overlapping) occurrences of `H` in a random text of
length `n`. For a text generated by a stationary Markov chain of order `1`, with transition matrix
`P` and stationary distribution `π`, Theorem 2.2 (i) gives, for `n ≥ m`,

  `E O_n(H) = P(H) (n - m + 1)`,  `P(H) = π(h_1) ∏_{j < m} P(h_j, h_{j+1})`  (equation (13)).

Theorem 2.2 assumes `n P(H) → ∞`, which for a fixed pattern and letter law means `P(H) > 0`. The
mean needs no such hypothesis: by stationarity each of the `n - m + 1` possible starts reads `H`
with probability `P(H)`. In the Bernoulli model (i.i.d. letters with law `q`) with `P(H) > 0` the
variance is exact for `n ≥ 2m - 1`: `Var O_n(H) = n c_1 + c_2` (equation (14) with
`E_1 = E_2 = 0`), where, with the autocorrelation polynomial
`A(z) = ∑_{k ∈ HH} P(h_{k+1} … h_m) z^{m-k}` over the set `HH` of `1 ≤ k ≤ m` such that the
length-`k` prefix of `H` equals its length-`k` suffix (Definition 2; `k = m` contributes `1`),

  `c_1 = P(H) (2 A(1) - 1 - (2m - 1) P(H))`,
  `c_2 = P(H) ((m - 1)(3m - 1) P(H) - (m - 1)(2 A(1) - 1) - 2 A'(1))`   (equations (15)–(16)).

Here a text of length `n` is a word `Fin n → V` with its exact probability, so expectations are
finite sums. Not recorded here: the generating functions of Theorem 2.1, the Markov variance with
its error term `O(r^{-n})` for an unspecified `r > 1`, and parts (ii)–(iv) of Theorem 2.2 (fixed
`r`, central and local limit, large deviations), whose hypotheses ("`n P(H) → ∞`" for a fixed
pattern, "`x = O(1)`") the paper does not state as uniformity conditions.

Sources: M. Régnier and W. Szpankowski, "On pattern frequency occurrences in a Markovian
sequence", Algorithmica 22 (1998) 631–649, doi:10.1007/PL00009244, Definition 2 and Theorem 2.2
(i), equations (13)–(16).
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Fin
public import Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Pi
public import Mathlib.Basic.Real.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Stochastic

@[expose] public section

namespace WantedExt.Biology.ComputationalBiology.PatternOccurrenceMomentsWanted

variable {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]

/-- `O_n(H)(w)`: the number of positions `i` with `i + m ≤ n` at which the word `w` of length `n`
reads `H`. -/
def occurrences {n m : ℕ} (H : Fin m → V) (w : Fin n → V) : ℕ :=
  ((Finset.range (n + 1 - m)).filter fun i =>
    ∀ j : Fin m, ∀ hj : i + j < n, w ⟨i + j, hj⟩ = H j).card

/-- `(π, P)` is a **stationary Markov chain** on `V`: `P` is a row-stochastic matrix and `π` a
probability vector with `π P = π`. -/
structure IsStationaryChain (π : V → ℝ) (P : V → V → ℝ) : Prop where
  /-- The transition matrix is row stochastic: its entries are nonnegative and each row sums to `1`
  (`Matrix.mem_rowStochastic_iff_sum`). -/
  stochastic : Matrix.of P ∈ Matrix.rowStochastic ℝ V
  /-- The initial law is nonnegative. -/
  pi_nonneg : ∀ a, 0 ≤ π a
  /-- The initial law has total mass `1`. -/
  pi_sum : ∑ a, π a = 1
  /-- The initial law is stationary. -/
  stationary : ∀ b, ∑ a, π a * P a b = π b

/-- **Chain weight of a word.** `π(w_0) ∏_{i + 1 < n} P(w_i, w_{i+1})` for a word `w` of length
`n ≥ 1`, and `1` for the empty word, for any real weights `π` and `P`. When `π` is a probability
vector and `P` a stochastic matrix (as under `IsStationaryChain π P`), it is the probability of
`w` under the chain started from `π`. -/
def markovProb (π : V → ℝ) (P : V → V → ℝ) : ∀ {n : ℕ}, (Fin n → V) → ℝ
  | 0, _ => 1
  | _ + 1, w => π (w 0) * ∏ i : Fin _, P (w i.castSucc) (w i.succ)

/-- **Product weight of a word.** `∏_i q(w_i)` for any real weights `q`. When `q` is a probability
vector (`q ≥ 0`, total mass `1`), it is the probability of `w` under i.i.d. letters with law `q`. -/
def bernoulliProb (q : V → ℝ) {n : ℕ} (w : Fin n → V) : ℝ :=
  ∏ i, q (w i)

/-- The **autocorrelation set** `HH`: the `1 ≤ k ≤ m` with `h_1 … h_k = h_{m-k+1} … h_m`. -/
def autocorrSet {m : ℕ} (H : Fin m → V) : Finset ℕ :=
  (Finset.Icc 1 m).filter fun k => ∀ j : Fin m, j.1 < k → ∀ hj : m - k + j < m,
    H j = H ⟨m - k + j, hj⟩

/-- **Tail weight.** `P(h_{k+1} … h_m) = ∏_{k ≤ j < m} q(h_{j+1})`, the product of the weights `q`
of the letters of `H` at the `0`-based positions `k, …, m - 1`. When `q` is a probability vector,
it is the Bernoulli probability of that tail of `H`. -/
def tailProb (q : V → ℝ) {m : ℕ} (H : Fin m → V) (k : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  ∏ j : Fin m, if k ≤ j.1 then q (H j) else 1

/-- **Autocorrelation polynomial at 1.** `A(1) = ∑_{k ∈ HH} P(h_{k+1} … h_m)`, the sum of the tail
weights `tailProb q H k` over the autocorrelation set. -/
def autocorrAt1 (q : V → ℝ) {m : ℕ} (H : Fin m → V) : ℝ :=
  ∑ k ∈ autocorrSet H, tailProb q H k

/-- **Derivative of the autocorrelation polynomial at 1.**
`A'(1) = ∑_{k ∈ HH} (m - k) P(h_{k+1} … h_m)`, with the tail weights `tailProb q H k`. -/
def autocorrDerivAt1 (q : V → ℝ) {m : ℕ} (H : Fin m → V) : ℝ :=
  ∑ k ∈ autocorrSet H, ((m - k : ℕ) : ℝ) * tailProb q H k

/-- **Exact mean in a stationary Markov text** (Régnier and Szpankowski 1998, Theorem 2.2 (i),
equation (13)): for a stationary Markov chain `(π, P)` on a finite alphabet, a pattern `H` of
length `m ≥ 1` and `n ≥ m`,
`∑_{w} P(w) O_n(H)(w) = P(H) (n - m + 1)` with `P(H) = π(h_1) ∏_{j < m} P(h_j, h_{j+1})`.

Source: Régnier and Szpankowski (1998), Theorem 2.2 (i). -/
public theorem_wanted expected_occurrences_markov (π : V → ℝ) (P : V → V → ℝ)
    (hc : IsStationaryChain π P) (m : ℕ) (hm : 1 ≤ m) (H : Fin m → V) (n : ℕ) (hn : m ≤ n) :
    ∑ w : Fin n → V, markovProb π P w * occurrences H w = markovProb π P H * (n - m + 1)

/-- **Exact variance in a Bernoulli text** (Régnier and Szpankowski 1998, Theorem 2.2 (i),
equations (14)–(16) with `E_1 = E_2 = 0`): for i.i.d. letters with law `q` (`q ≥ 0`, total mass
`1`) on a finite alphabet, a pattern `H` of length `m ≥ 1` with `P(H) = ∏_j q(h_j) > 0` (the
theorem assumes `n P(H) → ∞`, which for a fixed pattern means `P(H) > 0`) and every `n ≥ 2m - 1`,
the variance `E O_n² - (E O_n)²` equals `n c_1 + c_2` with
`c_1 = P(H)(2A(1) - 1 - (2m - 1)P(H))` and
`c_2 = P(H)((m - 1)(3m - 1)P(H) - (m - 1)(2A(1) - 1) - 2A'(1))`.

Source: Régnier and Szpankowski (1998), Theorem 2.2 (i). -/
public theorem_wanted variance_occurrences_bernoulli (q : V → ℝ) (hq : ∀ a, 0 ≤ q a)
    (hq₁ : ∑ a, q a = 1) (m : ℕ) (hm : 1 ≤ m) (H : Fin m → V) (hH : 0 < bernoulliProb q H)
    (n : ℕ) (hn : 2 * m - 1 ≤ n) :
    (∑ w : Fin n → V, bernoulliProb q w * (occurrences H w : ℝ) ^ 2) -
        (∑ w : Fin n → V, bernoulliProb q w * occurrences H w) ^ 2 =
      n * (bernoulliProb q H * (2 * autocorrAt1 q H - 1 - (2 * m - 1) * bernoulliProb q H)) +
        bernoulliProb q H * ((m - 1) * (3 * m - 1) * bernoulliProb q H -
          (m - 1) * (2 * autocorrAt1 q H - 1) - 2 * autocorrDerivAt1 q H)

end WantedExt.Biology.ComputationalBiology.PatternOccurrenceMomentsWanted
